A depictee can be the 'subject-matter' or the 'iconically
signified':

"This picture is about X" (subject matter)
"This picture looks like X" (iconically signified)

Texts usually don't iconically signify, but do have subject matter.
Branches don't have subject matter, but do iconically signify.
Pictures typically iconically signify their subject matter. So
'picture' is ambiguous. 

> Wouldn't the picture statement too be true whatever
> the picture? For that matter, couldn't we say that for
> every poem there is some world in which its subject
> matter is a Danish mermaid? Maybe we need some implicit
> restriction on worlds 

There surely are certain ontological restrictions, though I
don't know whether they are part of the language per se. For
example, I don't think that one thing can exist in the spacetime
of more than one world: if something is a dog in one world,
then it isn't a dog in any other world. Everything that is
a dog in this world exists in the spacetime of this world,
but not everything that is depicted in this world exists in
the spacetime of this world.

